window.CS_NARR=window.CS_NARR||{};window.CS_NARR["lld"]={"latlas": [[null, ["Hi, Buddy here. This sheet is about low level design: taking a problem like a parking lot, an elevator or a movie booking system, and turning it into clean classes, interfaces and working code. If high level design asks where the boxes go, low level design asks what's inside one box, and whether that code would survive the next requirement.", "There are two flavours of this interview. In the classic LLD round, you talk through a class design, draw a diagram and write the key methods in about an hour. In a machine coding round, you get ninety minutes to two hours and must produce code that actually runs. This sheet prepares you for both. Let's look at the map."]], ["lld-topic-atlas", ["The atlas has six areas. Interview method: the sixty minute route, the object design flow, the difference between entities, value objects and services, the machine coding time plan, a package layout and the evaluation rubric. OOP foundations: the four pillars, UML relationships, SOLID, broader design principles and GRASP, which tells you which class should own a responsibility. Design patterns: the creational, structural and behavioural families, modern enterprise patterns, the pairs people confuse, a pattern picker, and real code for strategy, state, observer and singleton. Classic problems: a playbook of common problems with their patterns and gotchas, and full code for the parking lot, LRU cache, rate limiter, elevator, movie booking and Splitwise. Concurrency: primitives, hazards, atomicity and the classic exercises. And code quality: API and method design, errors and edge cases, testing, and review questions."]], ["how-to-use-this-sheet", ["How to use it. If you're learning, start with OOP foundations, then the pattern catalogue. And with patterns, focus on when each one applies, the problem it solves, not just its definition. Anyone can recite \"the strategy pattern defines a family of algorithms\"; interviewers want to see you reach for it because the requirements say \"support several pricing rules\".", "To practise, take a problem from the playbook, set a ninety minute timer, and build a runnable solution with a small demo driver that exercises it. Actually run it. The bugs you hit under time pressure are exactly the ones you'll hit in the interview.", "Before a machine coding round, reread the time plan, the package layout and the rubric, then one worked design.", "And if the interview is tomorrow: the trigger phrases, the problem playbook, the concurrency hazards and the last minute checklist."]], ["what-interviewers-score", ["What do interviewers actually score? Five signals, and each maps to a part of this sheet.", "Clear entities and clear ownership: does each class have one job, and is it obvious who owns each piece of state? That's the responsibility guide and GRASP.", "Extensibility without edits: when a new requirement arrives, like a new vehicle type, do you add a class rather than editing five existing ones? That's SOLID and the pattern triggers.", "Working code early: do you get the core flow running before polishing? That's the time plan and package layout.", "Thread safety stated out loud: do you say what's shared and what protects it? That's the hazards and primitives.", "And edge cases and tests: do you name the tricky cases and show how you'd test them? That's the errors and testing cards."]]], "intro": [[null, ["Let's start with method: how to model behaviour, state and boundaries within an hour."]], ["60-minute-interview-route", ["Here's the sixty minute route.", "The first six minutes: use cases, constraints and edge cases. For a parking lot, ask: which vehicle types, how many floors, how are fees calculated, can a car take two spots, what happens when the lot is full? These answers shape the classes.", "Minutes six to twelve: entities, value objects and the invariants they protect. A ticket, a spot, a vehicle, a fee policy, and invariants like \"a spot holds at most one vehicle\".", "Twelve to eighteen: the public method signatures and who owns what. Write the signatures before any bodies: park takes a vehicle and returns a ticket; unpark takes a ticket and returns a receipt.", "Eighteen to twenty four: relationships and state transitions. How classes connect, and what states a spot or ticket moves through.", "Twenty four to forty four: implement the core path, and extend only as needed. This is the biggest block, and it's for working code.", "Forty four to fifty: concurrency and atomicity. What happens if two cars try to take the last spot at once?", "And the last ten minutes: errors, races, tests and extension points.", "What gets scored: entities with one job each, with invariants enforced inside them rather than by every caller. Patterns used for a real variation point, and named only after the code needs them. A working core path early, then extensions. Thread safety reasoned out loud: what's shared, and what guards it. Testability: an injected clock and ID generator, small interfaces, and named edge cases. And readable names, small methods, and no dead abstractions."]], ["object-design-flow", ["The object design flow, top to bottom. Start from use cases and edge cases. From them, find the invariants and decide who owns each piece of state. Then model entities and immutable value objects. Then define small public interfaces. Then services that orchestrate behaviour across entities. Only then add a pattern, and only where there's real variation. Then decide concurrency and transaction boundaries. And finally, test the state transitions, races and failures.", "Notice that patterns come late in this flow. A common beginner mistake is to start with \"I'll use a factory and a singleton here\" before knowing what the system does. Strong candidates start from the invariant, the rule that must never break, and let the structure follow."]], ["responsibility-guide", ["This guide tells you what each kind of building block owns, and it's the most useful card for avoiding god classes.", "An entity owns identity and lifecycle state. A booking has an ID and moves from held to confirmed to cancelled. Keep the operations that protect its invariants right next to that state: booking dot confirm should check that it's currently held, rather than every caller checking.", "A value object is immutable and compared by value, like Money, Address or a date range. Validate it once in the constructor, and then it's always valid. Two Money objects of a hundred rupees are equal, regardless of identity.", "A service orchestrates a use case, coordinating entities and gateways. The booking service holds seats, charges payment and confirms. Services are where workflows live, but don't let them grow into god classes that hold all the logic while entities become empty bags of data.", "A repository or gateway owns a boundary: persistence, or an external system like a payment provider. Depend on its interface, so tests can swap in a fake.", "And a strategy or state object holds a replaceable rule or the legal transitions. Use it when the variation is visible in the requirements, like several pricing rules, or behaviour that changes with status."]]], "oop": [[null, ["This section is the vocabulary interviewers expect while you model. A good habit is to name the principle at the moment you apply it: \"I'll keep the balance private and expose deposit and withdraw, so the invariant lives in one place; that's encapsulation.\""]], ["four-pillars-of-oop", ["The four pillars of object oriented programming, each with its interview cue.", "Encapsulation: state is private, and it changes only through methods that keep the invariants. The classic example is a bank account. Don't expose a public setter on the balance, because then any code anywhere can set it negative. Expose deposit and withdraw, and let withdraw refuse an overdraft. Now the rule lives in one place.", "Abstraction: callers depend on what an object does, not how it does it. A payment gateway interface with a charge method hides whether Stripe or Razorpay is behind it. Checkout code never changes when you switch providers.", "Inheritance: an \"is a\" relationship that reuses type and behaviour. A car is a vehicle. Use it only for true subtypes, and prefer composition when you just want to reuse code, because deep inheritance hierarchies become fragile.", "Polymorphism: one call, many implementations, chosen at runtime. The interview cue is replacing chains like \"if type equals car, do this; else if type equals bike, do that\" with subclasses or strategies, so each type knows its own behaviour and adding a type means adding a class."]], ["uml-class-relationships", ["UML relationships, from weakest to strongest, and how each looks in code.", "Dependency, drawn as a dashed arrow, is temporary use: a class uses another as a method parameter or local variable, and doesn't keep it.", "Association, a solid line, is a field holding a reference to another, independent object.", "Aggregation, a solid line with a hollow diamond, is a whole part relationship where the part outlives the whole. A team has players, but the players exist before and after the team, and are passed in from outside.", "Composition, a filled diamond, is stronger: the part dies with the whole. An order creates and owns its order lines, and deleting the order deletes them.", "Inheritance is a solid line with a hollow triangle pointing to the parent, as in a car extends a vehicle. Realisation, implementing an interface, is a dashed line with a hollow triangle, as in a card payment implements payment.", "And multiplicity: write numbers like one, zero or one, one or more, or many, on both ends of a relationship. They aren't decoration. They decide your collection types, a single field or a list, and whether you need null checks."]], ["solid-with-the-smell-and-the-fix", ["SOLID, with the smell that reveals each violation and the fix. Learning the smells is more useful than learning the definitions, because in an interview you'll spot smells in your own code.", "S, single responsibility: a class should have one reason to change. The smell is an invoice class that calculates totals, prints itself and saves itself to the database. Three reasons to change: tax rules, print layout and storage. Split it into a calculator, a printer and a repository. In a parking lot, the lot shouldn't compute fees; a fee calculator does, so fee rules can change without touching parking logic.", "O, open closed: open for extension, closed for modification. The smell is a switch on payment type that grows every time you add a payment method. The fix is an interface with one class per variant, so adding UPI means adding a UPI payment class, without touching checkout.", "L, Liskov substitution: a subtype must honour its parent's contract, so code using the parent works with any subtype. The famous smell is a square extending a rectangle. Setting a rectangle's width shouldn't change its height, but for a square it must, so code that works for rectangles breaks for squares. Another is a penguin extending bird, whose fly method throws an exception. The fix is to remodel the hierarchy, for example a separate Flyable interface that only flying birds implement.", "I, interface segregation: prefer small, client specific interfaces. The smell is classes implementing methods only to throw \"unsupported operation\". A simple printer forced to implement scan and fax. Split the fat machine interface into printer, scanner and fax, and let a multifunction device implement all three.", "D, dependency inversion: depend on abstractions, and have them injected. The smell is a service that creates its own MySQL repository inside a method, welding itself to MySQL. The fix is constructor injection of a repository interface. In production you pass the MySQL version; in tests you pass an in memory one."]], ["design-principles-beyond-solid", ["More principles, each with a one line meaning.", "Composition over inheritance: build behaviour from parts rather than deep class trees, which avoids the fragile base class problem, where a change in a parent breaks children in surprising ways.", "Tell, don't ask: tell an object to do something, like account dot withdraw, rather than asking for its balance and deciding outside. The decision belongs with the data.", "The Law of Demeter: talk only to your direct collaborators. A chain like order dot get customer dot get address dot get city means your code knows too much about other objects' insides.", "DRY, KISS and YAGNI: don't repeat knowledge, keep it simple, and you aren't going to need it, so don't build speculative features.", "High cohesion, low coupling: keep related behaviour together, with few, narrow dependencies between classes.", "Program to interfaces, and keep the domain free of frameworks and IO code, so business logic can be tested in isolation.", "Immutability for value objects and anything shared between threads; immutable objects are automatically thread safe.", "And fail fast: validate at construction time, and make illegal states unrepresentable, using enums and types instead of strings and flags."]], ["grasp-who-should-own-this-responsibility", ["GRASP answers the question you'll ask a hundred times in an LLD interview: which class should do this?", "Information expert: give the responsibility to the class that has the data needed. The order knows its lines, so the order computes its total.", "Creator: the class that contains or aggregates an object should create it. The order creates its order lines.", "Controller: a use case or facade object handles system events, not the user interface. The booking service handles \"book these seats\", not the button click handler.", "Polymorphism: when behaviour varies by type, put it in the types, not in a conditional.", "Pure fabrication: when no domain object naturally fits, invent a service class, like a repository or notifier. It's \"fabricated\" because it doesn't exist in the real world domain, and that's fine.", "Indirection: add an intermediary to decouple two classes, like an event bus between orders and notifications.", "And protected variations: wrap whatever is likely to change behind a stable interface, like external payment providers."]], ["language-essentials-for-lld-java-and-python", ["Language essentials, in Java and Python, with the interview cue for each.", "Interfaces versus abstract classes. In Java, an interface is a contract that a class can implement many of, with default methods allowed; an abstract class can hold shared state and code, but you can extend only one. In Python, use abstract base classes with abstract methods, or Protocol for structural typing, where anything with the right methods qualifies. The cue: an interface for a capability or a strategy, and an abstract class when subclasses share real state.", "Immutability. In Java: final fields, no setters, defensive copies of collections, and records for value objects. In Python: frozen dataclasses and tuples. Use them for value objects like Money and Address, and for anything shared across threads.", "Equality and hashing. In Java, always override equals and hash code together, because equal objects must have equal hashes. Python's frozen dataclasses generate both for you. And a classic bug: if you mutate a field used in the hash after putting the object in a hash map or set, the map can no longer find it.", "Enums: in Java, enums can carry fields and per constant behaviour; in Python, add methods to an Enum class. Model statuses and types as enums, never strings, so typos become compile errors.", "Generics: bounded types in Java, and the PECS rule, producer extends, consumer super; type variables in Python, which are hints only. They give you a repository of T, or a cache of K and V.", "Exceptions: Java has checked exceptions for recoverable conditions and unchecked ones for programming errors; Python's are all unchecked. Either way, define domain exceptions. Throwing seat unavailable is far clearer than returning null or minus one.", "Visibility: private by default in Java, a leading underscore by convention in Python, with properties for computed or read only attributes. No public mutable fields on entities.", "And composition: constructor injection in Java and passing collaborators to init in Python, with Optional for values that may be absent. This makes swapping and testing collaborators trivial."]]], "gof": [[null, ["Now the pattern catalogue: all twenty three Gang of Four patterns, plus the modern ones that show up in machine coding rounds. Here's the most important advice before we start. In an interview, use one or two patterns that the requirements actually call for, and say why. A design with seven patterns isn't impressive; it's a warning sign. Patterns are names for solutions to recurring problems, so the skill is recognising the problem."]], ["creational-5", ["The five creational patterns, which are all about how objects get made.", "Singleton ensures there's exactly one shared instance, like a configuration registry or an ID generator. Two cautions: make it thread safe, and make it injectable. A singleton that classes fetch through a global getter is hidden global state, which makes testing painful. Better to create one instance at startup and pass it in.", "Factory method lets a method or subclass decide which concrete class to create. For example, a vehicle factory takes a vehicle type and returns a car, bike or truck. Callers never write \"new car\", so adding a new type touches only the factory.", "Abstract factory creates families of related products that must match. A UI theme factory produces a dark button, a dark checkbox and a dark menu together, so you never mix themes by accident. Database driver families are another example.", "Builder constructs an object step by step, which shines when there are many optional parameters. Instead of a constructor with twelve arguments where half are null, you write: a pizza builder, with size large, add cheese, add olives, build. The result is usually immutable. HTTP request builders work the same way.", "And prototype creates new objects by cloning an existing one, useful when setup is expensive, like document templates or game units with many default settings."]], ["structural-7", ["The seven structural patterns, which are about how objects are composed.", "Adapter converts one interface into another that clients expect. You wrap a third party payment SDK, with its own odd method names, in an adapter that implements your payment gateway interface. Your code never sees the SDK directly.", "Bridge separates an abstraction from its implementation so both can vary independently. Shapes and renderers: instead of a class for every combination, like a red circle in OpenGL or a blue square in SVG, the shape holds a reference to a renderer. A TV remote and the device it controls is another example.", "Composite lets you treat a tree of parts uniformly. A folder contains files and other folders, but both can answer \"what's your size?\" A file returns its own size; a folder sums its children. Nested comments and menus work the same way.", "Decorator adds behaviour by wrapping an object in another with the same interface. A plain pizza wrapped in cheese wrapped in olives, each adding to the price. Or a stream wrapped in compression wrapped in encryption. Decorators stack, and each one adds a little.", "Facade provides a simple front for a complicated subsystem. A booking service facade with one book method hides the seat service, payment service and notification service behind it.", "Flyweight shares intrinsic, unchanging state among many objects to save memory. In a chess engine, there's one shared \"white knight\" object for its type and appearance, and each square just references it. Text glyphs and map tiles work the same way.", "And proxy is a stand in that controls access to the real object, with the same interface. Lazy loading an image only when it's displayed, caching results, checking permissions, or a remote stub that hides a network call."]], ["behavioral-11", ["The eleven behavioural patterns, which are about how objects interact and divide responsibilities.", "Strategy swaps algorithms behind an interface: pricing rules, matching rules, split types in Splitwise, cache eviction policies. It's the most commonly used pattern in LLD interviews by far.", "State changes an object's behaviour when its internal state changes. A vending machine behaves differently when idle, holding money or dispensing. An order lifecycle, an ATM session. Instead of if statements on a status field, each state is a class.", "Observer notifies subscribers when something changes: stock price alerts, order status updates, UI events.", "Command turns a request into an object you can store, queue, log or undo. Undo and redo in an editor, job queues, macro recording.", "Chain of responsibility passes a request along a chain of handlers until one handles it, or each handles part of it. Log levels, approval flows where a manager approves small amounts and a director large ones, middleware pipelines, and an ATM dispensing notes of different denominations.", "Template method fixes the skeleton of an algorithm in a base class and lets subclasses fill in specific steps. A board game turn, generating different report formats, importing different file types.", "Iterator traverses a collection without exposing its internals, including paginated results from an API.", "Mediator centralises complex interactions between objects. A chat room relays messages so users don't hold references to each other; air traffic control coordinates planes so they don't talk directly.", "Memento captures and restores an object's state without breaking encapsulation, for editor undo and game save points.", "Visitor adds new operations to a fixed structure of classes without changing them. Evaluating or pretty printing a syntax tree, or computing tax or exports over a set of shapes.", "And interpreter evaluates sentences in a small grammar, like a rule engine or a simple expression parser."]], ["modern-and-enterprise-patterns", ["Modern and enterprise patterns, the ones you'll actually use in most production code.", "Dependency injection, always: pass collaborators in rather than creating them inside. It's what makes everything else testable.", "Repository hides storage behind a collection like interface: find by ID, save, delete. Your services don't know whether it's Postgres or an in memory map.", "Unit of work tracks several changes and commits them atomically.", "Specification makes business rules composable objects, like \"is premium and has spent over ten thousand\", combined with and, or and not. Great for eligibility checks and filters.", "Null object avoids null checks by providing a do nothing implementation, like a \"no discount\" discount that always returns zero.", "Object pool reuses expensive objects like database connections and threads.", "Producer consumer decouples creating work from executing it, through a queue.", "An event bus, or publish subscribe, gives many to many asynchronous notifications.", "A circuit breaker stops calling a failing dependency for a while.", "And a rule engine stores rules like coupons, fraud checks and pricing as data, so the business can change them without a deploy."]], ["patterns-people-confuse", ["These pairs are confused constantly, and interviewers like asking about them. Let's untangle each.", "Strategy versus state: both swap behaviour behind an interface. The difference is who switches. With strategy, the client picks the algorithm. With state, the state object itself decides the next state; idle moves itself to has money.", "Decorator versus proxy: both wrap an object with the same interface. A decorator adds behaviour and decorators stack. A proxy controls access to the object, for laziness, caching or security, and you usually have just one.", "Adapter versus facade: an adapter converts one interface to a different one. A facade simplifies many interfaces behind a new simple one.", "Factory method versus abstract factory: one product, often chosen through inheritance, versus a whole family of matching products created through a factory object.", "Builder versus factory: a builder assembles one complex object step by step; a factory picks and creates a type in a single call.", "Observer versus publish subscribe: in observer, the subject knows its observers directly. In pub sub, a broker sits in between, so publishers and subscribers don't know each other, and delivery is often asynchronous.", "Template method versus strategy: inheritance fills in steps of a fixed algorithm, versus composition swapping the whole algorithm.", "Composite versus decorator: both wrap the same interface, but a composite has many children, while a decorator wraps exactly one.", "Mediator versus observer: a mediator coordinates two way logic between participants; an observer only broadcasts one way.", "Bridge versus adapter: a bridge is designed up front so abstraction and implementation vary independently; an adapter is retrofitted to fix a mismatch between existing interfaces.", "Command versus strategy: a command is a request object you can queue, log or undo; a strategy is an interchangeable algorithm.", "Flyweight versus singleton: flyweight shares many immutable instances, one per key; singleton has exactly one instance.", "And chain of responsibility versus decorator: a link in a chain may stop the request and not pass it on; a decorator always delegates to the wrapped object, adding behaviour around it."]], ["trigger-phrase-pattern", ["Requirements often contain trigger phrases that point straight at a pattern. Listen for them.", "\"Support multiple types, and we may add more later\" points to strategy plus a factory to pick the strategy.", "\"Behaves differently when idle, running or out of stock\" points to state.", "\"Notify users when\" something happens points to observer.", "\"Undo\", \"replay\" or \"queue requests\" points to command, perhaps with memento for undo.", "\"Escalate\", \"levels\" or \"fall through\" points to chain of responsibility.", "\"Folders contain files and folders\" points to composite.", "\"Add ons\", \"toppings\" or \"wrap with logging\" points to decorator.", "\"Integrate a legacy or external API\" points to adapter.", "\"Exactly one\" of something points to singleton, though dependency injection of a single instance is usually better.", "And \"an object with many optional fields\" points to builder."]], ["which-pattern-a-quick-picker", ["This picker asks questions in order about what varies in the requirements. Does an algorithm or rule vary? Strategy. Does behaviour depend on state? State. Do other parts need to react to changes? Observer. Do you create objects by type? Factory. Do you add features by wrapping? Decorator. Do you need undo or queued requests? Command. And if the answer to all of these is no: plain classes, no pattern. That last answer matters. Interviewers penalise patterns used for show, and \"I considered a pattern here but plain classes are simpler\" is a mature thing to say."]], ["vending-machine-state-diagram", ["This is the vending machine's state diagram, which is worth drawing in any state based problem. It starts idle. Inserting a coin moves it to \"has money\", and inserting more coins keeps it there, adding to the balance. Selecting a product moves it to dispensing, but only if the product is in stock and the balance covers the price. Asking for a refund from \"has money\" returns the money and goes back to idle. And after dispensing the product and returning change, it's idle again.", "Here's the key idea: every arrow that isn't in the diagram is an illegal transition. Selecting a product while idle, or inserting a coin while dispensing. Your code should reject those loudly, not silently ignore them. Drawing the diagram first makes the illegal transitions obvious."]], ["code-smells-and-the-refactoring-that-fixes-them", ["Code smells and the refactoring that fixes each one. These are worth knowing because interviewers sometimes ask you to critique code, and because you'll catch them in your own.", "A god class has dozens of methods and fields across unrelated concerns. Extract classes by responsibility, and if callers need one entry point, put a facade in front that delegates.", "A long method needs comments to explain its sections. Extract a method for each step, and let the method names replace the comments.", "A switch on type, repeated in several places, means every new type requires edits in all of them. Replace the conditional with polymorphism, using strategies or subclasses.", "Primitive obsession: money as a double, IDs as raw strings, status as an int. Introduce value objects and enums. Money as a double is a real bug, because floating point can't represent point one exactly.", "Feature envy: a method that mostly reads another object's data. Move it to the class that owns the data.", "Data clumps and long parameter lists: the same four or five arguments passed together everywhere. Introduce a parameter object or a builder.", "Shotgun surgery: one change requires edits in many classes. Move related behaviour together, so each decision lives in one place.", "An anemic domain model: entities are bags of getters and setters, and all logic sits in services. Move the behaviour that protects invariants into the entities.", "Hidden dependencies: objects created with \"new\", or singletons fetched, deep inside methods. Use constructor injection behind interfaces.", "And speculative generality: abstract layers and patterns with only one implementation. Inline them until a second variation actually appears. That's YAGNI in action."]], ["strategy-factory-java", ["This Java code combines strategy and factory for ride pricing. Let me walk through it.", "There's a pricing strategy interface with one method, price, which takes a ride and returns Money. Notice it returns Money, a value object, not a double.", "Standard pricing implements it by multiplying the ride's kilometres by twelve. Surge pricing holds a multiplier, set in its constructor, and charges twelve per kilometre times that multiplier.", "Then there's a small pricing factory with a static method, for demand. It takes the demand to supply ratio, and returns surge pricing if the ratio is above one point five, otherwise standard pricing.", "The comment at the end is the interview line. The ride service depends only on the pricing strategy interface, which is dependency inversion. And a new pricing rule, say airport pricing, is a new class implementing the interface, with no changes to existing code, which is the open closed principle. When you present code like this, say those two sentences out loud."]], ["state-machine-vending-machine", ["This is the vending machine using the state pattern, and it's elegant once you see the trick.", "The State interface declares three operations, insert coin, select and dispense, and gives each a default implementation that throws an illegal state exception. So by default, every action is illegal.", "Each concrete state then overrides only the actions that are legal in that state. Idle overrides insert coin: it adds the coin to the machine's balance and switches the machine to the has money state.", "Has money overrides two actions. Insert coin just adds to the balance. Select checks the inventory, throwing out of stock if the product isn't available, then checks the balance against the price, throwing insufficient funds if it's short. If both pass, it records the selection and switches to dispensing.", "Dispensing overrides only dispense: it releases the product, returns change, and switches back to idle.", "The beauty is in the last comment. Illegal transitions fail loudly by default, because they hit the throwing default. And there's no if else chain on a status enum anywhere. Adding a new state, like maintenance mode, is a new class."]], ["observer", ["This is the observer pattern for order events. A subscriber interface has one method, on event, taking an order event. The order publisher keeps a list of subscribers, with subscribe to add one and publish to notify them all in a loop.", "The interesting detail is the list type: a copy on write array list. Every time someone subscribes, it copies the underlying array, which sounds wasteful, but it means publish can iterate safely even if another thread subscribes at the same moment, with no locks and no concurrent modification exceptions. It's ideal when reads, here publishing, vastly outnumber writes, here subscribing.", "And the last comment: if delivery should be asynchronous, so a slow subscriber doesn't hold up the publisher, hand events to an executor service or a blocking queue instead of calling subscribers directly."]], ["thread-safe-singleton", ["Three ways to make a thread safe singleton in Java.", "First, the holder idiom. The ID generator has a private constructor and a private static nested holder class that contains the single instance. The JVM only loads the holder class the first time get is called, and class loading is guaranteed thread safe. So you get lazy initialisation and thread safety with no locks at all. It's the best general answer.", "Second, the enum singleton: an enum with a single constant called instance. It's also safe against reflection and serialisation, which can otherwise create a second instance. Joshua Bloch recommends it.", "Third, double checked locking: check if the instance is null, lock, check again, then create. It only works correctly if the field is volatile, because without that, another thread might see a partly constructed object. It's a classic interview question precisely because it's easy to get wrong.", "And in Python, a module level instance is already a singleton, since modules are imported once.", "But remember the earlier caution: even a perfect singleton is global state. Prefer creating one instance and injecting it."]]], "patterns": [[null, ["This section maps the classic problems to their patterns, and to the one gotcha each problem hides. The gotcha is what separates a decent answer from a strong one."]], ["lld-problems-pattern-the-gotcha", ["Let me go through them, explaining why each gotcha matters.", "Parking lot. Patterns: strategy for spot allocation, a factory, maybe a singleton for the lot. The gotcha: delegate down a hierarchy. The parking lot delegates to floors, floors delegate to spots. If the lot does everything itself, you've built a god class.", "Rate limiter. Strategy for the algorithm. The gotcha: a token bucket with lazy refill, so you compute the new tokens from elapsed time when a request arrives, instead of running a timer thread. And a concurrent hash map of buckets, one per user.", "Tic tac toe. The gotcha: a constant time win check using counters for each row, column and diagonal, instead of scanning the board after every move.", "Vending machine. State pattern. The gotcha: no if else chains on state; polymorphic state classes.", "Splitwise. Strategy for split types, equal, exact and percentage. The gotcha: settling up minimises cash flow with a greedy algorithm over net balances.", "BookMyShow. Strategy and observer. The gotcha: separate the physical seat from the show seat, the seat for one particular showing, and lock show seats with an expiry.", "Chess. Template method and factory. The gotcha: each piece validates its own movement geometry, like a bishop moving diagonally, while the board validates the path is clear and the king isn't left in check.", "Snake and ladder. The gotcha: one Jumper class, with a start and an end, covers both snakes and ladders. A snake jumps down, a ladder jumps up, and the code doesn't care.", "Elevator. Strategy and state. The gotcha: a SCAN or LOOK sweep using sorted sets of stops, not a first in first out queue, which would make the elevator zigzag.", "Comment system. Composite. The gotcha: store a materialised path, like one slash four slash nine, so fetching a whole thread is one query.", "Hotel management. Strategy for pricing. The gotcha: availability is a date range overlap check.", "ATM. State, and chain of responsibility for the cash dispenser. The gotcha: an external bank service does the atomic debit; the ATM doesn't own account balances.", "LRU cache. The gotcha: a hash map plus a doubly linked list with dummy head and tail nodes. Know the wiring cold, because you'll write it under pressure.", "Food delivery. Strategy for dispatch, observer for status updates. The gotcha: focus on the core models and the order lifecycle rather than everything.", "Locker. Strategy. The gotcha: size ordering, so a small package fits a small, medium, large or extra large locker, but a large package fits only large or extra large.", "Notification service. Strategy, observer and factory. The gotcha: you can add a WhatsApp channel without touching the core.", "Coupon system. Strategy or a rule engine. The gotcha: coupons are database rows turned into strategies at runtime, never hard coded classes per coupon.", "Logger. Chain of responsibility, strategy and producer consumer. The gotcha: a blocking queue for asynchronous writing, so logging never slows the request path.", "Library. The gotcha: Book versus BookItem, the title versus the physical copies.", "Order management. Saga or command. The gotcha: an order orchestrator with compensating steps.", "Ride sharing. Strategy for matching and pricing, state for the ride. The gotcha: treat the location service as a black box.", "Pub sub. Observer. The gotcha: a blocking queue per topic plus a worker.", "And inventory. The gotcha: track available, reserved and purchased quantities separately, because a reservation isn't yet a sale."]], ["pattern-trigger-map-use-at-most-2", ["This is the trigger map again in compact form, with one firm rule in its title: use at most two patterns. Multiple algorithms, pricing or payment types: strategy. Creating objects by type: factory. Notify on change: observer. A single shared instance like config or a registry: a thread safe singleton. Many optional parameters: builder. Adding behaviour dynamically, like toppings or filters: decorator. Behaviour that depends on state, like a vending machine, ATM or order: state. Undo or queued requests: command. Passing through handlers, like log levels or approvals: chain of responsibility. And a tree of parts, like folders or comments: composite."]], ["solid-scoring-signals", ["The scoring signals in one place. The five SOLID rules in one breath: one reason to change, extend by adding a class, subtypes are substitutable, small interfaces, depend on abstractions. Composition over inheritance, with a facade service as the entry point. Immutable value objects like Money and Address, and enums for statuses. Thread safety stated explicitly, with no global mutable state.", "And keep this recovery line ready if you get stuck or over complicate things: \"Let me simplify. I'll get the core flow working, then extend.\" Interviewers respect that far more than a half built abstraction, because it's exactly what good engineers do on the job."]], ["class-sketch-parking-lot", ["This class diagram sketches the parking lot. The parking lot has two public methods: park, which takes a vehicle and returns a ticket, and unpark, which takes a ticket and returns a fee. The lot has many floors, shown by the one to many multiplicity. Each floor can find a spot of a given type, and has many spots. Each spot knows its type and whether it's free. The lot also holds a pricing strategy, which computes the fee from a ticket, and creates tickets with an ID and entry time.", "Notice how responsibilities line up with GRASP. The floor finds spots because it has the spots; that's information expert. The fee rule is behind a strategy, so changing from hourly to flat pricing doesn't touch the lot; that's protected variations."]]], "lldprob": [[null, ["For each classic problem: the classes to draw first, the methods to write first, the patterns, and the part interviewers probe. Then real code for six of them."]], ["problem-playbook", ["This playbook is the biggest card on the sheet, so I'll take it in groups. For each problem, listen for the core entities, the key methods, and above all, what interviewers probe.", "Parking lot. Entities: parking lot, floor, spot with a type, vehicle, ticket, fee calculator and gate. Methods: park takes a vehicle and returns a ticket; unpark takes the ticket and returns a receipt. Patterns: strategy for allocation and fees, a factory, a singleton. They probe concurrent spot allocation: two cars at two gates wanting the last compact spot. Keep a free list per spot type, and make taking a spot from it atomic.", "Elevator. Entities: elevator system, elevator, request, direction, dispatcher and door. Methods: request a floor with a direction, and step, which advances the simulation. Patterns: strategy for dispatch, state for moving, idle and maintenance. They probe the scheduling algorithm, SCAN or LOOK, and choosing the nearest car already heading the right way.", "Movie booking. Entities: movie, theatre, screen, show, seat, show seat, booking and payment. Methods: hold seats for a show, and confirm a booking. Patterns: strategy for pricing, observer for notifications. They probe holding seats with an expiry, and no double booking.", "Splitwise. Entities: user, group, expense, split with equal, exact and percent variants, and a balance sheet. Methods: add expense, balances for a user, and settle. Patterns: strategy and factory. They probe validating that splits sum to the total, and simplifying debts using heaps on net balances."]], ["problem-playbook", ["Next group.", "Vending machine. Entities: machine, state, inventory, product and coin. Methods: insert coin, select, dispense and refund. Pattern: state. They probe illegal transitions and making change.", "ATM. Entities: ATM, card, account, transaction, cash dispenser and bank service. Methods: authenticate and withdraw. Patterns: state, and chain of responsibility for note denominations, where the five hundred handler dispenses what it can and passes the remainder to the two hundred handler, and so on. They probe atomically debiting and dispensing, and reversing the debit if the cash fails to come out.", "Library management. Entities: book, book item for each physical copy, member, loan, reservation and fine. Methods: check out, return and reserve. Patterns: observer for availability alerts, strategy for fines. They probe the book versus book item distinction, and borrowing limits per member.", "Chess. Entities: game, board, cell, piece with a subclass per type, move and player. Methods: make move from one cell to another, and is checkmate. Patterns: template method, factory, and command for undo. They probe the split of validation between pieces and the board.", "Tic tac toe on an n by n board. Entities: game, board, player and move. Method: move at a row and column, returning the result. They probe the constant time win check.", "Snake and ladder. Entities: game, board, jumper, player and dice. Method: play turn. Pattern: strategy for the dice, so you can swap in a loaded die for tests. They probe the single jumper class."]], ["problem-playbook", ["Next, the infrastructure style problems.", "LRU and LFU cache. Entities: cache, node, doubly linked list, and frequency buckets for LFU. Methods: get and put in constant time. Pattern: strategy for the eviction policy. They probe the map and linked list wiring, tracking the minimum frequency for LFU, and thread safety.", "Rate limiter. Entities: rate limiter, bucket, rule and clock. Method: allow, taking a client ID and returning true or false. Pattern: strategy for the algorithm. They probe lazy refill, per key locking so one busy client doesn't block others, and an injectable clock for tests.", "Logger. Entities: logger, level, appender for console or file, and formatter. Method: log a message at a level. Patterns: chain of responsibility, strategy, singleton and producer consumer. They probe the asynchronous appender with a bounded queue, and level filtering.", "Pub sub or a message queue. Entities: broker, topic, partition, message, publisher, subscriber and offset. Methods: publish to a topic, and subscribe. Pattern: observer. They probe per subscriber offsets, ordering, and back pressure.", "Hotel booking. Entities: hotel, room with a type, booking, guest, date range and invoice. Methods: search by date range and type, book and cancel. Pattern: strategy for pricing. They probe the date range overlap check and holds."]], ["problem-playbook", ["And the final group.", "Ride sharing. Entities: rider, driver, trip with states, location, matcher and pricing. Methods: request ride, accept and complete. Patterns: strategy, state and observer. They probe matching the nearest available driver atomically, so one driver isn't assigned two trips.", "Food delivery. Entities: restaurant, menu, order with states, cart, delivery agent and dispatcher. Methods: place order, assign agent and track. Patterns: state, strategy and observer. They probe the order lifecycle, and reassigning when an agent rejects.", "File system. Entities: an entry, which is a file or directory, a path and permissions. Methods: make directory, list, and find with a filter. Patterns: composite for the tree, and specification for search filters. They probe recursive size, and search filters composed with and or or, like \"larger than one megabyte and ending in dot log\".", "Task scheduler. Entities: task, trigger for once or cron schedules, scheduler, worker and a delay queue. Methods: schedule a task at a time, and cancel. Patterns: command and strategy. They probe a min heap ordered by next run time, a worker pool, and retries.", "Shopping cart and inventory. Entities: product, cart, cart item, inventory, order and coupon. Methods: add, checkout and apply coupon. Patterns: strategy and specification. They probe reserved versus purchased stock, and coupon stacking rules.", "Notification service. Entities: notification, channel for email, SMS or push, template and preference. Method: send to a user for an event. Patterns: strategy, factory and observer. They probe adding a channel without touching the core, retries and deduplication.", "Stack Overflow. Entities: user, question, answer, comment, vote, tag and reputation. Methods: post, vote and accept. Patterns: observer and composite. They probe one vote per user, and reputation updates.", "And a cricket scoreboard. Entities: match, innings, over, ball, player and scorecard. Method: record ball, taking an event. Patterns: observer for live updates, command for each ball so you can undo a mistake. They probe the rules: extras like wides and no balls that don't count as legal deliveries, wickets, and strike rotation after odd runs and at the end of an over."]], ["lru-cache-o-1-python", ["This is the LRU cache with constant time get and put, in Python. Let me take you through it carefully, because it's asked constantly.", "First, a Node class with four slots: key, value, previous and next. Using slots saves memory per node.", "The cache stores its capacity and a dictionary from key to node. Then the clever bit: two sentinel nodes, head and tail, linked to each other. Real nodes always live between them. Because head and tail always exist, inserting and removing never has to check for an empty list or a missing neighbour. That's what removes most of the bugs.", "Two helpers do all the pointer work. Remove unlinks a node by pointing its previous node's next to its next node, and its next node's previous to its previous node. Add to front inserts a node right after head: the node's next becomes head's old next, its previous becomes head, then head's old next points back to the node, and head points forward to it.", "Get: if the key isn't in the dictionary, return minus one. Otherwise, take the node, remove it, add it to the front, because it was just used, and return its value.", "Put: if the key already exists, remove the old node. Create a new node, store it in the dictionary, and add it to the front. Then, if the dictionary has grown beyond capacity, the least recently used node is the one just before tail. Remove it from the list, and delete its key from the dictionary.", "That last step is why each node stores its own key: when you evict from the list, you need the key to delete the dictionary entry.", "And the closing comment: to make it thread safe, guard get and put with one lock, since even get modifies the list. For more throughput, split the key space into shards, each with its own lock."]], ["token-bucket-rate-limiter-java-thread-safe", ["This is a thread safe token bucket in Java.", "The bucket has a capacity, a refill rate expressed per nanosecond, the current number of tokens, the time of the last refill, and a clock. The clock is injected, so tests can control time instead of sleeping. The constructor starts the bucket full.", "Try acquire is synchronized, so only one thread updates a given bucket at a time. It reads the current time, and refills lazily: it adds the elapsed nanoseconds times the rate, capped at the capacity, and records the new last time. There's no background thread topping up buckets; the refill is calculated only when a request arrives. Then, if there's less than one token, it returns false. Otherwise it spends one token and returns true.", "The rate limiter keeps a concurrent hash map from client ID to bucket. Allow uses compute if absent to create a bucket on a client's first request, atomically, so two simultaneous first requests can't create two buckets. Then it calls try acquire.", "Notice the locking granularity: each bucket has its own lock, so one busy client never blocks others.", "The follow ups to know. Leaky bucket smooths output to a fixed rate. Fixed window is simple but bursts at window edges. Sliding log is exact but stores every timestamp; a sliding window counter approximates it with two counters. To make it distributed, keep the bucket in Redis and do refill and take in one Lua script, so it stays atomic. Bound memory by expiring idle clients' buckets. And respond with status four twenty nine and a retry after value computed from the token deficit, how long until a token will be available."]], ["parking-lot-class-sketch-java", ["This is the parking lot in Java, and it shows how the earlier principles turn into code.", "Enums for spot types, motorbike, compact and large, and vehicle types, bike, car and truck. The ticket is a record, which makes it an immutable value with an ID, spot ID, vehicle type and entry time.", "Two interfaces carry the variation. A spot allocator allocates a spot for a vehicle type, returning an optional spot, and releases spots. A fee policy computes the fee for a ticket at an exit time. These are the seams where requirements will change.", "The parking lot class is a facade holding the allocator, the fee policy and a concurrent map of active tickets.", "Park asks the allocator for a spot. If the optional is empty, it throws a lot full exception. The comment says the allocation must be atomic inside the allocator, which is where the concurrency risk lives: two gates must never get the same spot. Then park creates a ticket with a random UUID and the current time, stores it in the active map, and returns it.", "Unpark removes the ticket from the active map. If nothing was there, it throws invalid ticket. Removing in one atomic step is subtle but important: the same ticket can't be used to exit twice, even concurrently. Then it releases the spot and returns a receipt with the fee from the fee policy."]], ["tic-tac-toe-with-o-1-win-check-java", ["Tic tac toe with a constant time win check. Here's the trick, and it's a favourite.", "Instead of scanning the board after every move, the class keeps an array of counters for rows, another for columns, and two single counters for the main diagonal and the anti diagonal.", "On each move, player one adds one and player two subtracts one. So the move updates its row counter and its column counter. If the row equals the column, the move is on the main diagonal, so update that counter. If the row plus the column equals n minus one, it's on the anti diagonal, so update that.", "Now, if any of those counters reaches n, player one must have filled that entire line, because only their moves add one. If it reaches minus n, player two did. So the check after each move just looks at four numbers, whatever the board size.", "Validation, like rejecting an occupied cell, the wrong player's turn, or moves after the game is over, lives in a Game class wrapped around this, keeping this class small and focused."]], ["logger-with-chain-of-responsibility-java", ["A logger built as a chain of responsibility.", "There's a level enum: debug, info, warn and error. An abstract handler holds a reference to the next handler. Its then method sets the next handler and returns it, so you can chain handlers fluently. The handle method writes the message if this handler accepts the level, and then passes it to the next handler, if there is one. Subclasses implement two methods: accepts, and write.", "The console handler accepts info and above, and prints. The alert handler accepts only errors, and pages the on call engineer.", "So you build a chain: a console handler, then an alert handler. An error goes to both; an info goes only to the console.", "One thing worth saying in an interview: this chain passes every message to every link. The classic chain of responsibility stops at the first handler that handles the request. Both are valid; say which one you're building and why.", "The follow ups: for throughput, put a bounded blocking queue and a single writer thread in front, an asynchronous appender, and decide whether to block or drop messages when the queue is full. Make levels configurable per package at runtime, without a restart. Formatting is a strategy, and additional sinks like files or the network are just more handlers in the chain. And for thread safety, either use the single writer thread or synchronise each sink's write."]], ["in-memory-pub-sub-with-per-subscriber-offsets-ja", ["This is an in memory publish subscribe system with offsets per subscriber, a tiny version of Kafka.", "The topic is an append only log, a copy on write list of messages. Publish appends. Read at an offset returns the message there, or null if the subscriber has caught up.", "Each subscriber is a runnable with its own atomic offset, a handler, and a volatile running flag. Its run loop reads the message at its current offset. If there isn't one yet, it parks the thread for a millisecond and tries again; a real system would use wait and notify instead of polling. Otherwise, it handles the message, and only then increments its offset. Incrementing after handling is \"commit after processing\", which gives at least once delivery: if the handler crashes, the message is read again.", "Reset offset lets a subscriber replay from any point, which is one of the great strengths of a log over a queue. And stop clears the running flag; it's volatile so the loop thread sees the change.", "The closing comment captures the design: because each subscriber keeps its own position, slow subscribers don't block fast ones, and any subscriber can replay."]]], "lldworked": [[null, ["Now three full walkthroughs and a booking flow: requirements, the class diagram, the code interviewers actually probe, and the extensions to mention. The code is in Java, since most machine coding rounds expect it, but the ideas carry to any language."]], ["elevator-system", ["Let's design an elevator system. The core is two pieces: a scheduler inside each car, using the LOOK algorithm, which keeps going in one direction while stops remain that way, and a dispatcher that picks the cheapest car for each hall call.", "The class diagram: an elevator system with request pickup, taking a floor and direction; request stop, taking a car and a floor; and tick, which advances time. It holds many elevators and one dispatcher. The dispatcher is an interface with a choose method, implemented by a least cost dispatcher. Each elevator has its current floor, its direction, and two sorted sets, stops above and stops below. And direction is an enum: up, down or idle.", "Requirements to confirm: N cars and M floors. Hall calls, a floor plus the direction you want, versus car calls, a destination pressed inside the car. Minimise wait time without starving anyone. Doors, capacity and maintenance mode can come later as extensions. And a single controller process, driven by tick, which makes it a deterministic simulation you can test.", "Key decisions. Two tree sets per car, stops above and stops below, so finding the next stop in the current direction is logarithmic. The dispatcher is a strategy, so you can swap nearest car, least cost or zoning without touching the elevators. And the car's own state, moving, doors open or maintenance, can grow into a state pattern if needed.", "Now the code. Add stop is synchronized. If the floor is above the car, it goes into the up set; below, into the down set. And if the car was idle, it starts moving towards that floor.", "Step, also synchronized, moves one floor per tick. If the car is going up and there are no more stops above, it turns: down if there are stops below, otherwise idle. If there are stops above, it moves up one floor, and if that floor was a stop, removes it and opens the doors. Going down is the mirror image. That's LOOK: it only reverses when there's nothing left ahead, rather than travelling to the end of the shaft like SCAN.", "The cost function, used by the dispatcher, is where judgement lives. An idle car's cost is simply its distance to the call. A moving car's cost is its distance if the call is on its way, meaning same direction and ahead of it. Otherwise it's the distance plus a large penalty, a hundred, because serving it means turning around. The least cost dispatcher just picks the car with the minimum cost.", "Edge cases to test. A call for the floor the car is already at while its doors are open. An opposite direction hall call on the car's path, like someone on floor five wanting to go down while the car goes up past it; serve it only after the car turns. Every car busy or in maintenance; queue the call, never drop it. Repeated presses of the same button should be idempotent; a sorted set handles that naturally. And an emergency stop or overweight car: hold it and re dispatch its hall calls to other cars.", "Follow ups to expect. A hundred floors and eight cars: zone the building, or add express cars that skip lower floors. Testing the scheduler: drive it with a fake clock and tick, so every run is deterministic. Destination dispatch: riders enter their floor in the lobby, so the system can group people going to the same floors.", "And extensions to mention: skip hall calls when the car is full; in the morning rush, park idle cars at the lobby; age old requests so far floors aren't starved; and since requests arrive on many threads, guard each car's state with its own lock, which is what those synchronized methods do."]], ["movie-ticket-booking-bookmyshow", ["Movie ticket booking. Two ideas carry the design: model the seat for each show separately from the physical seat, and make holding seats all or nothing, with an expiry.", "The class diagram: a show has a movie, a screen and a start time. A seat has an ID and a type, like recliner or regular. A show seat links a seat to one show, with a status and a price. A booking has an ID, a user, a list of show seats and a status. The booking service has hold, taking a show, seat IDs and user, and confirm, taking a hold and payment. And a seat lock provider interface with lock and unlock.", "Requirements to confirm: search shows by city and movie, view the seat layout, select up to some maximum number of seats, hold them for ten minutes, pay, then confirm or release. No double booking under concurrent requests, and prices by seat type and show.", "Key decisions. Seat versus show seat: the physical seat is shared across every show on that screen, so status can't live on the seat; it lives on the show seat. Locking sits behind an interface: in memory for the interview, Redis or the database in production. Pricing is a strategy, like weekday, premium or surge, and notifications use observer. And money is stored as integer minor units, paise or cents, never a double.", "Now the in memory seat lock, which is what interviewers probe. It keeps a map from show to a map from seat to lock, where each lock records the user and an expiry. The clock and the time to live are injected.", "Lock first gets, or creates, the seat map for this show. Then it synchronises on that show's map only. That's a deliberate choice: two people booking different shows never wait for each other, but two people booking the same show are serialised.", "Inside the lock, it makes two passes. The first pass checks every requested seat. If any seat has a lock that hasn't expired and belongs to a different user, it returns false immediately, without changing anything. Only if every seat passes does the second pass lock all of them with the new expiry. That two pass structure is what makes it all or nothing: you never end up holding three of the four seats you wanted.", "Unlock removes only locks owned by this user, so one user can't release another's hold.", "The confirm flow. Check that every seat is still locked by this user and the lock hasn't expired. Charge payment with an idempotency key. Mark the show seats booked and save the booking in one transaction. Then unlock and publish a booking confirmed event. If payment fails or times out, release the lock and mark the booking failed.", "Extensions: in production, use a Redis set if not exists with an expiry per seat, with a unique constraint in the database as the safety net. A waitlist using observer, notifying people when seats free up. And coupons as a rule engine."]], ["splitwise-expense-sharing", ["Splitwise. Three ideas: split types are a strategy with validation, balances are a ledger, and settling up is greedy matching of debtors and creditors.", "The class diagram: an expense service with add expense, balances for a user, and a settle plan for a group. An expense has an ID, who paid, the amount in cents and a list of splits. A split is abstract, with a user and an amount. A split strategy interface computes splits from an amount, the users and details, implemented by equal, exact and percent splits. And a balance sheet applies expenses and reports each user's net amount.", "Requirements to confirm: users, groups, and expenses paid by one person and split equally, by exact amounts or by percentages. Show who owes whom, simplify debts into as few transfers as possible, and record settlements.", "Key decisions. Store money as whole cents in a long, never a double. When an amount doesn't divide evenly, give the remainder cents to the first participants, deterministically, so the result is reproducible. Each strategy validates its input: exact amounts must sum to the total, percentages to a hundred. Balances are a net amount per user per group: positive means you're owed money, negative means you owe. And expenses are immutable; an edit is a reversal plus a new expense, which preserves an audit trail.", "The equal split code: divide the amount by the number of users to get a base, and take the remainder. Then each user gets the base, plus one extra cent if their index is less than the remainder. For a hundred cents split three ways, that's thirty four, thirty three and thirty three. The splits always sum exactly to the amount, which is the invariant.", "Simplifying debts. Build two max heaps: creditors, ordered by how much they're owed, and debtors, ordered by how much they owe. Repeatedly pop the largest creditor and the largest debtor. Transfer the smaller of the two amounts from the debtor to the creditor. Whoever still has a remainder goes back into their heap. Continue until a heap is empty. Each round settles at least one person completely, so there are at most n minus one transfers.", "And the fact to mention: finding the true minimum number of transfers is NP hard, because it involves finding subsets of people whose balances cancel out. Greedy is the practical answer, and saying that shows you know the theory."]], ["booking-flow-as-a-sequence-diagram", ["This sequence diagram shows the booking flow end to end. The user asks the booking service to hold seats for a show. The booking service asks the seat lock provider to lock them for this user, and gets back a lock that expires in ten minutes, so it returns a hold ID to the user.", "Later, the user confirms with the hold ID and a payment token. The booking service checks the seats are still locked by this user. It charges the payment gateway with an idempotency key, and gets success. It saves the booking and marks the seats booked through the repository. It unlocks the seats, and tells the user the booking is confirmed.", "Why draw this? Draw it right after the class diagram, because it proves your classes can actually carry out the use case; every arrow is a method you've defined. And it shows exactly where the concurrency risk sits: in the gap between locking and confirming, where the lock might expire or payment might time out."]]], "concurrency": [[null, ["Concurrency is where many LLD answers fall apart. Let's be precise about atomicity and error paths."]], ["correctness-boundary", ["For each kind of shared state problem, there's a mechanism and the invariant it protects.", "A concurrent map update, like \"create a bucket if absent\": use an atomic map operation like compute if absent, or a lock. The invariant: check and set happen as one operation, so two threads can't both see \"absent\".", "A shared counter: an atomic integer, or a synchronized allocator. The invariant: no increments are lost or duplicated.", "A transition that changes several fields, like moving a booking from held to confirmed while recording the payment: a lock or a database transaction. The invariant: no other thread ever sees a half updated state.", "A work queue: a blocking queue with a bound. The invariant: back pressure and shutdown behaviour are defined, rather than memory growing forever.", "A retryable command: an idempotency key with a stored result. The invariant: a retry returns the same logical outcome rather than doing the work twice.", "And an external side effect, like charging a card: an outbox or a saga boundary. The invariant: any failure can be retried or compensated."]], ["review-questions", ["Six review questions to ask of any design, including your own before you hand it in. Who owns each mutable field? Can two requests race on the same object? What makes each method atomic? Can a retry duplicate an operation? Are the terminal states and invalid states explicit? And can each dependency be replaced in a unit test? If you can answer all six crisply, your design is in good shape."]], ["optimistic-vs-pessimistic-locking-in-code", ["Optimistic versus pessimistic locking, in code.", "Pessimistic locking assumes conflicts will happen, so it locks first. Synchronize on the account for the whole read, modify, write, and debit inside the lock. Nobody else can touch the account meanwhile.", "Optimistic locking assumes conflicts are rare, so it doesn't lock; it detects conflicts and retries. In memory, that's a compare and swap loop. An atomic reference holds the current balance, which is an immutable value. In a loop: read the current balance, compute the next one by subtracting the amount, and compare and set. Compare and set only succeeds if the reference still holds the value you read. If another thread changed it first, it fails, and you loop and try again with the fresh value.", "In the database, optimistic locking uses a version column. Update the balance and increment the version, but only where the ID matches and the version is still the one you read. If zero rows were updated, someone else got there first, so re read and retry.", "When to use which? Optimistic wins when conflicts are rare, because there's no locking overhead. Pessimistic wins when contention is high or retries are expensive, because constant retrying wastes work.", "Two traps. Deadlock under pessimistic locking: if a transfer from A to B locks A then B, while a transfer from B to A locks B then A, they can wait for each other forever. Fix it by always acquiring locks in a global order, for example by account ID, or by using try lock with a timeout.", "And the ABA problem with compare and swap: a value changes from A to B and back to A, and your compare and swap succeeds even though things changed underneath. Use a versioned reference, like Java's atomic stamped reference, which compares a stamp as well as the value."]]], "conc2": [[null, ["Machine coding rounds increasingly add \"now make it thread safe\". Know the primitive, the hazard it prevents, and the classic exercises."]], ["primitives-java-and-python", ["The concurrency primitives, in Java and Python.", "Mutual exclusion: synchronized or a reentrant lock in Java, which adds try lock and fairness options; a Lock or a reentrant RLock in Python.", "Many readers, one writer: a read write lock, or a stamped lock, in Java. Python has no built in read write lock, so you build one from a lock and a condition.", "Limiting concurrent access, like at most ten connections: a semaphore in both languages.", "Waiting for a condition: condition variables with await and signal, or wait and notify all, in Java; a Condition or an Event in Python.", "A lock free counter: atomic integer or long adder in Java, which use compare and swap. In Python you need a lock, because the global interpreter lock doesn't make plus equals atomic; it's a read, an add and a write, and a thread switch can happen between them.", "Visibility: volatile and the happens before rules in Java, so one thread's writes are seen by another.", "A concurrent map: concurrent hash map with its atomic compute and merge methods in Java; a dictionary plus a lock in Python.", "A hand off queue: blocking queues, or a delay queue, in Java; queue dot Queue and a priority queue in Python.", "Waiting for N tasks: a countdown latch, cyclic barrier or phaser in Java; a barrier, or concurrent futures wait, in Python.", "And thread pools: executor services, completable futures and virtual threads from Java twenty one; thread pool and process pool executors, or asyncio, in Python.", "One Python fact to state clearly: because of the global interpreter lock, threads help with IO bound work, but CPU bound work needs processes. Free threaded builds exist from Python three thirteen, but they're optional."]], ["hazards-and-how-to-prevent-them", ["The concurrency hazards, what each looks like, and how to prevent it.", "A race condition: check then act, or read, modify, write, where another thread slips in between the steps. Use an atomic operation like compare and swap or compute if absent, or one lock around both steps.", "Deadlock: two threads each hold a lock the other needs, and both wait forever. Use a global lock order, try lock with a timeout, or fewer locks.", "Livelock: threads keep politely yielding to each other and make no progress, like two people in a corridor stepping the same way. Use randomised back off.", "Starvation: a thread never gets the lock because others keep winning. Use fair locks and bounded priorities.", "A visibility bug: one thread sets a stop flag, and another never sees it, looping forever, because the value is cached in a CPU register. Use volatile, locks or atomics.", "A lost update: two writers overwrite each other's changes. Use an optimistic version check or a row lock.", "ABA: a compare and swap succeeds on a recycled value. Use versioned or stamped references.", "And priority inversion: a low priority thread holds a lock that a high priority thread needs, while medium priority threads keep the low one from running. Use priority inheritance and keep critical sections short.", "Finally, the Coffman conditions: deadlock needs all four of mutual exclusion, hold and wait, no preemption, and circular wait. Break any one, and deadlock is impossible. Usually you break circular wait by ordering lock acquisition."]], ["classic-concurrency-exercises", ["The classic concurrency exercises, and the core idea of each.", "Producer consumer with a bounded blocking queue: one lock and two conditions, not full and not empty, and always wait in a loop that rechecks the predicate.", "Readers and writers: a read write lock, and a decision about whether readers or writers get preference, since one side can starve the other.", "Dining philosophers: five philosophers, five forks, each needing two. Either number the forks and always pick up the lower numbered one first, which breaks circular wait, or use a semaphore to let at most four sit at once.", "Print in order, FooBar and zero even odd: semaphores or condition hand offs between threads, so each thread signals the next.", "Building water molecules: semaphores allowing two hydrogen threads and one oxygen thread, plus a barrier so they bond together.", "A multithreaded web crawler: a concurrent visited set, a work queue and a thread pool. The tricky part is stopping: you're done only when the queue is empty and no task is still in flight, because an in flight task might add more URLs.", "A thread safe LRU cache: one lock, or split the key space into shards with a lock each.", "And a delayed task scheduler: a priority queue ordered by deadline, with a condition wait that sleeps until the earliest deadline, or until a new, earlier task arrives."]], ["bounded-blocking-queue-python", ["This is a bounded blocking queue in Python, a perfect interview exercise.", "The constructor creates a deque, records the capacity, creates one lock, and builds two conditions on that same lock: not full and not empty. Sharing one lock is important; both conditions guard the same queue.", "Enqueue enters the not full condition, which acquires the lock. While the queue is at capacity, it waits, which releases the lock and sleeps until signalled. When there's room, it appends the item, and notifies not empty, waking a consumer that might be waiting.", "Dequeue is the mirror image. It enters the not empty condition, and while the queue is empty, waits. Then it pops from the left, notifies not full to wake a waiting producer, and returns the item.", "The comment marks the crucial detail: while, not if. When a thread wakes up, the condition might no longer be true. Another thread may have grabbed the item first, or the wake up may be spurious, which the language allows. So always recheck the condition in a loop after waking. Using if instead of while is the classic bug interviewers look for."]], ["producer-consumer-with-a-bounded-queue", ["This diagram shows producer consumer with a bounded queue. Two producers put work into a blocking queue with a fixed capacity, and two consumers take work out. When the queue is full, producers block until there's space. When it's empty, consumers wait until work arrives.", "The bound is what gives you back pressure: a fast producer can't run ahead and exhaust memory; it's forced to slow down to the consumers' pace. And to stop consumers cleanly at shutdown, send a poison pill, a special message that means \"stop\", one per consumer, or set a shutdown flag and wake them."]]], "machine": [[null, ["Machine coding rounds last ninety minutes to two hours and need runnable code. They reward a clean structure, a working demo and easy extension much more than clever algorithms."]], ["time-plan-90-min", ["The ninety minute plan.", "The first ten minutes: clarify scope, and write down the four to six must have commands, like park, unpark, status. Agree on them with the interviewer.", "Ten to twenty: the models, meaning enums, value objects and entities, plus the interfaces.", "Twenty to sixty five: services for the happy path, using in memory repositories. Get something running end to end as early as possible, even if it's simple.", "Sixty five to eighty: the driver or demo that runs the sample input, plus validation and exceptions.", "And the last ten: demonstrate an extension point, like adding a new vehicle type in two minutes, add a note on concurrency, and clean up.", "Notice that the happy path comes before error handling. A running program with basic validation beats a beautifully validated program that doesn't run."]], ["package-layout", ["A package layout that interviewers recognise immediately. A model package for entities, value objects and enums. A repository package with interfaces and in memory implementations. A service package for use cases, one facade per aggregate. A strategy package for pluggable rules like pricing and matching. An exception package for domain exceptions. And a main driver that parses commands and prints output.", "Two rules make it clean. Keep all input and output in the driver; services return values and never print. And one public method per use case, so the service API reads like the requirements."]], ["evaluation-rubric", ["The evaluation rubric, the things graders actually tick off. It runs end to end on the sample input. Concerns are separated, with no god class. It's extensible, so a new type means a new class. Names are meaningful, methods are small, and there's no dead code. Edge cases and errors are handled explicitly. Thread safety is called out wherever state is shared. And there are tests, or at least a demo, that prove the behaviour."]], ["api-and-method-design", ["Method design. Methods are verbs in the domain's language, like reserve and settle, not generic names like process or handle. Return domain objects or result types, never raw maps that callers have to decode. Validate inputs at the boundary, and let constructors enforce invariants, so an invalid object can't exist. Prefer immutable inputs and outputs, records in Java and frozen dataclasses in Python. Make methods idempotent where retries are possible, by accepting a request ID. And give list methods pagination and filtering from the start."]], ["errors-and-edge-cases", ["Errors and edge cases. Use domain exceptions, like seat unavailable or insufficient funds, rather than generic ones; the exception name documents the business rule. Decide between exceptions, optionals or result types, and be consistent across the codebase. Check the usual suspects: empty input, duplicates, not found, capacity full, invalid transitions, concurrent conflicts, and partial failure. And on partial failure, roll back or compensate; never leave half updated state, like a seat marked booked with no booking record."]], ["testing-the-design", ["Testing is where dependency inversion pays off. Because you injected interfaces, you can pass fakes for the clock, the repository and the payment gateway, and test without real time, databases or networks.", "Unit test each strategy, and each state transition, including the illegal ones. Use table driven tests for rules like fees, splits and discounts: a table of inputs and expected outputs, run through one test. Write a concurrency test where many threads try to book the same seat, and assert exactly one succeeds.", "And even if time runs out, say which tests you would write. Naming them still earns credit."]]], "last": [[null, ["Finally, the checklist for the last few minutes before your round. Let me go through it slowly."]], ["lld-last-minute-checklist", ["Confirm the use cases before drawing any classes.", "State the invariants, and who owns each piece of state.", "Separate entities, immutable values, services and gateways.", "Define method signatures before implementation details.", "Use composition, and at most two patterns that earn their place.", "Make state transitions explicit, and invalid transitions explicit too.", "State thread safety and transaction boundaries out loud.", "And dry run five kinds of case: normal, duplicate, invalid, concurrent and failure.", "That's the whole sheet. You now have the method, the principles, the patterns, the problems and the code. In the room, start simple, get it running, then extend. You've got this."]]]};
